        "thinking": "Now I have the full picture. Routes in `server/src/app.ts` that are NOT in `route-registry.ts`:\n\n1. `companySkillRoutes(db)` — uses raw Express handlers, not `expressHandler`\n2. `pluginRoutes(...)` — plugin routes, complex dependencies\n3. `adapterRoutes()` — adapter routes, complex dependencies\n4. `accessRoutes(db, ...)` — access/member routes, uses raw Express handlers\n\nRoutes that ARE in `route-registry.ts`:\n\n\n- `companyRoutes(db, storage)` → `/api/companies`\n- `agentRoutes(db, {})` → `/api`\n- `assetRoutes(db, storage)` → `/api`\n- `projectRoutes(db)` → `/api`\n- `issueRoutes(db, storage, {})` → `/api`\n- `issueTreeControlRoutes(db)` → `/api`\n- `routineRoutes(db, {})` → `/api`\n- `environmentRoutes(db, {})` → `/api`\n- `executionWorkspaceRoutes(db)` → `/api`\n- `goalRoutes(db)` → `/api`\n- `approvalRoutes(db, {})` → `/api`\n- `secretRoutes(db)` → `/api`\n- `costRoutes(db, {})` → `/api`\n- `activityRoutes(db)` → `/api`\n- `dashboardRoutes(db)` → `/api`\n- `userProfileRoutes(db)` → `/api`\n- `sidebarBadgeRoutes(db)` → `/api`\n- `sidebarPreferenceRoutes(db)` → `/api`\n- `inboxDismissalRoutes(db)` → `/api`\n- `instanceSettingsRoutes(db)` → `/api`\n- `llmRoutes(db)` → `/api`\n- `authRoutes(db)` → `/api/auth`\n\nI'm noticing that two route handlers are missing from the Cloudflare setup: the skills routes and the adapter routes.\n\nLooking at what I've already added to the extra routes file, I've got a few endpoints for skills and access management, but there are many more routes I need to handle. I'm seeing that the full implementations are spread across separate files—one for skills, one for access and member management, one for adapters, and one for plugins. I need to decide whether to wire these up through the existing route handler or add them individually to the extra routes file.\n\nThe challenge is that these route files use raw Express handlers rather than the wrapped format that the extraction mechanism expects, so I can't just pull them through automatically. I could either refactor those files to use the wrapper format, but that's a massive undertaking across the server package, or I could implement CF-native versions of all these routes. Given the scale—access management alone is over 3700 lines with tons of endpoints—I'm wondering if there's a way to make the extraction mechanism work without rewriting everything.\n\nExamining how the extraction function works... I need to understand what `expressHandler` actually does and whether I can adapt raw Express handlers to work with it. The real issue is that `expressHandler` wraps handlers to tag them for extraction, but the raw handlers in `access.ts` are calling `res.json()` and `res.status()` directly—they're not returning `Response` objects. I could create a `cfHandler` wrapper that registers directly on the Hono app, but that's essentially what `cf-extra-routes.ts` already does. The core question is whether I need to convert all these raw Express handlers or if there's a way to make them work as-is in Cloudflare mode.\n\nSo what actually works in a CF Workers context? Database queries work through Hyperdrive, file storage through R2, and static assets through Workers Assets. But adapters and plugins fundamentally can't work because they depend on Node.js processes. Sidecar calls might work if configured, and real-time events work over WebSocket but need Durable Objects for pub/sub. This means the CRUD endpoints that just do database and storage operations should work fine, while anything involving execution or runtime features is out of scope for CF.\n\nNow I need to go through the actual routes in access.ts, company-skills.ts, and the others to see what we're dealing with.\n\nLooking at the access.ts routes, I see a lot of member management endpoints—listing, updating, and deleting members; managing invites; handling join requests with approve/reject actions; and various onboarding and diagnostic endpoints. Most of these are straightforward database operations.\n\nFor company-skills.ts, there's skill CRUD with some already completed, plus file management and sync routes that interact with the filesystem. The adapters.ts file has install, update, and delete operations that need npm and process management alongside database changes. The plugins.ts routes are just starting to come into view.\n\nI'm realizing this is a substantial amount of work, so I need to be strategic about what's feasible in Cloudflare. The key insight is that database-only operations can live in CF, but anything touching the filesystem, running npm, or managing processes needs to stay in Node.js or return a 501. Looking at what actually matters for the UI, the members and settings pages are hitting the most endpoints, so those should be my priority for implementation. I need to check the instance settings page as well to see what additional 501 endpoints are being hit there.",
